    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Here Are Some of the Serious and Obscure Brands That EICMA 2026 Had To Offer – Worth Knowing About\nMassive Buick Encasa Minivan Has 713 Imaginary HP and Rocks the World of North American CGI MPVs\n23 photos\nPhoto: jlord8 / Instagram\nIf you want the latest news about General Motors in relation to its Buick brand, the marque has a sleek new hybrid sedan dubbed Buick Electra GS with two plug-in hybrid powertrains, but it’s not for North America.\nInstead, at home, General Motors is reportedly planning to spend no less than $750 million to electrify the Chevrolet Traverse, Buick Enclave, and GMC Acadia with hybrid versions , according to Reuters. All three SUVs with three rows are produced at GM’s Lansing Delta Township plant in Michigan, and the company has so far acknowledged it has plans to retool the factory for hybrid production, without actually revealing the HEV models it plans.\nThe decision could impact a lot of customers: a variety of buyers are growing more concerned about fuel costs, so it’s logical that GM is looking to broaden its powertrain lineup after previously investing heavily in EVs that are out of favor in the US while leaving hybrids to popular brands from Asia or Europe. Better late than never, of course, so that course of action got people thinking – including the members of the imaginative guild of digital car content creators dwelling across the parallel universes of vehicular CGI, apparently.\nFor example, Jim, a virtual artist known as \" jlord8 \" on social media, loves messing around with all things CGI from the automotive realm, and he dreams of a Buick minivan to add to the new and upcoming hybrid roster from GM. In his latest unofficial, hypothetical design project, we find a cool headline: “Buick is building a 713-hp minivan that makes the GNX look slow.”\nOf course, he doesn’t want a fight with his followers, and thus he further explains: “I mean a high horsepower van would be awesome stateside, but I guess the cool Buicks are only for Asia now. And nothing makes the GNX look slow.” So, he took the Chinese Buick Electra Encasa minivan introduced at the 2025 Guangzhou Auto Show and adapted it with the attitude of an American land yacht and a proposed 713-hp powertrain, most likely of the PHEV variety rather than a full EV.\nWhile this project is merely wishful thinking, a Buick minivan isn’t a bad idea per se. The US automaker has become a crossover SUV -only brand in America with the Envista, Encore GX, Envision, and Enclave on sale. A Buick Encasa would fit right in with the moniker series, and maybe it’s not a bad idea to compete with the Chrysler Pacifica, Toyota Sienna, Honda Odyssey, and the Kia Carnival across the US minivan sector, which is experiencing a return to form as of late.\nSo, what do you think? Would you buy something like this or not? View this post on Instagram A post shared by Jim (@jlord8)\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nBuick Encasa Buick Encasa Hybrid MPV CGI minivan rendering jlord8\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT This 1958 Chevrolet Yeoman Is No Longer Original, You'll Certainly Like the Upgrades",
